---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
const P = '/misc/irvine-oc/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Irvine and Around — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Orange County close to home, mostly through my wife's walks with Wangcai: Crystal Cove sunsets, Heisler Park in Laguna, the headlands at Dana Point, the Wedge, Upper Newport Bay, Huntington's dog beach, the hills of Irvine, and the harbor lights at Christmas.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2022–2026</p>
  <h1>Irvine and Around</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Orange County close to home, mostly on my wife's walks with Wangcai.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>35 outings · November 2022 – November 2026</strong><br />
    Crystal Cove · Heisler Park and the Montage lawn in Laguna Beach · the Pageant of the Masters · the Dana Point headlands ·
    the Wedge, Upper Newport Bay and Castaways Park in Newport · Huntington Dog Beach · the hills of Irvine ·
    Cook's Corner and Lago Santa Margarita · harbor lights at Christmas<br />
    most of them short walks, many at sunset · nearly all with Wangcai
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-oc.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-oc.webp'} alt="Map of coastal Orange County marking Huntington Dog Beach, Upper Newport Bay, Balboa Island, the Wedge, Crystal Cove, Heisler Park in Laguna Beach, the Montage lawn, Dana Point, the hills near Bommer Canyon, Irvine Spectrum, Cook's Corner and Lago Santa Margarita." /></a>
    <figcaption>The places in this post. Each stop is placed where its photo was taken.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Most of the photos in this post are my wife's, from walks with Wangcai near where we live in Orange County. Since
    Wangcai <a href="/misc/wangcai-2025">came home in January 2025</a>, my wife has taken her out to the coast and the
    trails around Irvine again and again, often just for an hour around sunset. Many of those walks were just the two of
    them, and I say so below. Where I was there too, I write "we".
  </p>
  <p>
    None of these were trips. They are the places a short drive away: a state park on the cliffs, a park above the
    tide pools in Laguna, a harbor, a marsh, a dog beach and a few ridges. Below they are grouped by place rather than
    by date, from Crystal Cove down the coast to Dana Point, then back up through Newport and Huntington Beach, inland to
    Irvine's hills, and finally to the lights in December.
  </p>

  <h2 id="crystal-cove">Crystal Cove</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Crystal Cove State Park</strong>, between Newport and Laguna, is the place my wife's photos return to most.
    The first visit in her library is in November 2023, before Wangcai: an afternoon on the terrace of the Shake Shack
    above the cove, ending with the sun going down into the ocean at 4:46 pm.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crystal-cove-2023-sunset.webp'} alt="The sun setting into the ocean in an orange band of sky, with a calm sea below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset from Crystal Cove, November 19, 2023, 4:46 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From 2025 on, the visits are walks with Wangcai along the bluff trail above the beach. In May 2025 the trail
    looked down on a gray-green sea and the sage along the edge; in June she stood at the top of the bluff at dusk,
    looking down at the waves and the long curve of the beach.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crystal-cove-bluff.webp'} alt="A view from a bluff over a sage bush to the ocean, with a boat far out on the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bluff trail, May 18, 2025, 4:36 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crystal-cove-wangcai.webp'} alt="Wangcai, a cream-colored dog on a pink leash, seen from behind at the edge of a bluff, looking down at the beach and the waves at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai on the bluff, June 2, 2025, 8:07 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In September 2025 my wife took her back to the Shake Shack terrace for the sunset, and again in May 2026, the
    summer I spent in the Bay Area. The view from there looks down over the palms and cottage roofs to the sea.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crystal-cove-sept-sunset.webp'} alt="The sun just above the horizon over the ocean, with a palm at the right edge and rooftops below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>September 19, 2025, 6:48 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crystal-cove-2026-sunset.webp'} alt="The sun setting behind a headland, with tall palms in silhouette and cottage roofs below them." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>May 29, 2026, 7:53 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="laguna">Laguna Beach</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Heisler Park</strong> is a strip of lawn and palms on the cliffs at the north end of downtown Laguna Beach,
    with paths down to coves and tide pools. My wife has walked it many times. In July 2025 she sat on the lawn with
    Wangcai and pointed the camera straight up at the palms.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'laguna-palms.webp'} alt="Three tall palm trees seen from directly below against a blue sky with white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Palms over the lawn at Heisler Park, July 11, 2025, 7:33 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Two days later we were in Laguna together for the <strong>Pageant of the Masters</strong>, the summer show in which
    the cast poses as living re-creations of famous artworks. The only photo here is the stage before the show began.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pageant-stage.webp'} alt="An outdoor stage with a red curtain inside a large white frame, set against a dark hillside, with the audience filling the seats in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Pageant of the Masters stage before the show, July 13, 2025, 8:33 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In September 2025 she was there at sunset, with the palms and the rocks in silhouette against a sky going from blue
    to orange.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'heisler-sunset.webp'} alt="Palm trees and a rocky point in black silhouette against an orange and blue sunset sky over the sea." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Heisler Park at sunset, September 12, 2025, 7:02 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In May 2026 she came by in the late morning, when the water in the coves was clear enough to see the rocks under it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'heisler-cove.webp'} alt="Tall palms on a cliff above a cove of turquoise water and a small beach." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A cove below the park, May 19, 2026, 11:21 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'heisler-tidepool.webp'} alt="Ledges of tan sandstone running into clear blue water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The rocks at the water's edge, 11:34 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In January 2026 we went together, with Wangcai, and took a stairway down the cliff to the water.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'laguna-stairs.webp'} alt="Wangcai on a concrete stairway with metal railings going down a cliff toward the sea, which glitters in the sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Down to the water in Laguna, January 23, 2026, 2:59 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Further south, the lawn in front of the <strong>Montage</strong> hotel runs along the top of the bluff. My wife
    walked Wangcai there at sunset in September 2026.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'montage-palms.webp'} alt="A row of palms and a gnarled tree in silhouette against an orange afterglow, above a dark lawn and path." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Montage lawn after sunset, September 3, 2026, 7:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="dana-point">Dana Point</h2>
  <p>
    Dana Point is the far end of these walks. In October 2025 my wife and Wangcai spent the late afternoon in the park
    above <strong>Lantern Bay</strong>, until the sun went down behind a line of yellow flowers on the slope.
    In January 2026 she went up to the overlook on the <strong>headlands</strong>, which looks down on the long jetty of
    Dana Point Harbor and the boats inside it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dana-sunset.webp'} alt="The sun setting over the ocean between two palms, with yellow flowers in the foreground and clouds above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lantern Bay, October 10, 2025, 6:13 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dana-headlands.webp'} alt="A view from a green headland over a long stone jetty and a harbor full of boats, with hills beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The harbor from the headlands, January 25, 2026, 1:43 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="newport">Newport Beach</h2>
  <p>
    The oldest photo here is mine: a single frame of palms against the sunset at <strong>Fashion Island</strong>, the
    day before Thanksgiving 2022. In April 2025 I took a few of the <strong>West Jetty</strong> at the mouth of
    Newport Harbor, where the path runs along the rocks next to the channel.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fashion-island-2022.webp'} alt="Palm trees silhouetted against a dusk sky over a lit roundabout and lawn." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fashion Island, November 23, 2022, 5:19 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'west-jetty.webp'} alt="A paved path beside a rock jetty along the harbor channel, with palms and a large house behind and boats across the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The West Jetty, April 16, 2025, 3:00 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A few weeks later, in May 2025, we took Wangcai to <strong>the Wedge</strong>, the beach at the tip of the Balboa
    Peninsula, at sunset. She sat on the sand and watched the waves. In June 2026 my wife went back to the peninsula on
    her own with Wangcai for another sunset, this time near the Balboa Pier.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wedge-wangcai.webp'} alt="Wangcai on a pink leash, seen from behind, sitting on footprinted sand and looking out at the waves." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai at the Wedge, May 9, 2025, 7:44 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wedge-2026.webp'} alt="The sun setting at the end of a long beach, with waves rolling in and a pier in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The peninsula at sunset, June 30, 2026, 8:01 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From <strong>Castaways Park</strong>, on a bluff above the harbor, the whole of Newport Harbor lies below, with the
    towers of Newport Center behind it. My wife's photo was taken on a February evening in 2026.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'castaways.webp'} alt="A view over Newport Harbor full of boats and docks, with yellow wildflowers in front and office towers and hills behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Newport Harbor from Castaways Park, February 14, 2026, 5:53 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The place she walks Wangcai most in Newport is <strong>Upper Newport Bay</strong>, the estuary behind the harbor,
    with dirt paths along its edge. Her photos from there run from a December morning to a July sunset over the marsh.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'back-bay-wangcai.webp'} alt="Wangcai walking along a dirt path through green brush above the water of the bay." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>December 6, 2025, 9:54 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'back-bay-sunset.webp'} alt="The sun setting over the channels of a marsh, with dark brush in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>July 6, 2026, 7:40 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On an October evening in 2026, Wangcai stood next to a sign marking the path as hiking only: no bikes and no horses.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'back-bay-sign.webp'} alt="Wangcai standing next to an orange sign with symbols for no horses and no bikes and the words Hiking only." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>October 27, 2026, 6:10 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="huntington">Huntington Beach</h2>
  <p>
    Huntington Beach has <strong>Dog Beach</strong>, a stretch of sand where dogs can be off leash, with a grassy bluff
    path above it. My wife took Wangcai there in November 2025. On the sand Wangcai sat and watched the surf.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hb-dog-beach.webp'} alt="A path and a wooden fence along a grassy bluff above a wide beach, with palms and gulls in a hazy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bluff path, November 1, 2025, 2:57 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hb-wangcai-waves.webp'} alt="Wangcai on a pink leash sitting on wet sand, looking at the surf." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai and the surf, 3:11 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The most recent photo in this post is from the same coast, two days before I wrote it: by 2:30 that afternoon Wangcai
    had been buried in the sand up to her neck.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hb-buried.webp'} alt="Wangcai's head sticking out of a mound of sand, eyes half closed." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Huntington Beach, November 7, 2026, 2:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="irvine">The hills of Irvine</h2>
  <p>
    Irvine itself has its own open space: wide dirt trails over the hills on the south side of the city, around
    <strong>Quail Hill</strong> and <strong>Bommer Canyon</strong>. These are my wife's evening walks with Wangcai, and
    the photos follow the seasons: golden grass in September, green in March, dry again by June.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bonita-trail.webp'} alt="Wangcai walking ahead on a wide dirt trail between dry grasses, with hills and haze in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>September 6, 2025, 6:29 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bommer-ridge.webp'} alt="Wangcai on a dirt ridge trail through green grass and wildflowers, with the city spread out below under a gray sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>March 31, 2026, 6:28 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In June 2026, while I was in the Bay Area, she kept up the evening walks; by then the grass on the ridge had dried
    to gold again.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'ridge-sunset.webp'} alt="Wangcai on a dirt trail beside a wooden fence in golden evening light, with dry grass hills behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>June 3, 2026, 6:54 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Some evenings the sky did more than the trail. On September 21, 2025 the clouds over the hills turned a deep orange
    a few minutes before seven. A week later, on September 28, we were both out at dusk when a SpaceX Falcon 9 lifted
    off from Vandenberg Space Force Base, far up the coast, and its exhaust trail curved across the sky over a shopping
    center parking lot.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'orange-sky.webp'} alt="Bright orange and red clouds over a dark line of trees and hills." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>September 21, 2025, 6:58 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rocket.webp'} alt="A long white, twisting rocket exhaust trail across a dusk sky above trees and a row of lit shops." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Falcon 9 trail, September 28, 2025, 7:09 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In February 2026 my wife found a shadier walk: a creek trail in Lake Forest, under trees, along a rocky streambed.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'creek-trail.webp'} alt="Wangcai sitting on rocks and fallen branches in a shaded creek bed, mouth open." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A creek trail in Lake Forest, February 24, 2026, 1:17 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="inland">Cook's Corner and Lago Santa Margarita</h2>
  <p>
    One Sunday in June 2025 we drove inland instead. <strong>Cook's Corner</strong> is a roadside bar at the foot of the
    Santa Ana Mountains where motorcyclists gather, and that afternoon the lot was packed with rows of bikes under the dry
    hills. An hour later we were at <strong>Lago Santa Margarita</strong>, the lake in the middle of Rancho Santa
    Margarita, with geese on the shore and the town's houses around the water.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cooks-corner.webp'} alt="Rows of large motorcycles parked under brushy hills on a sunny day." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Motorcycles at Cook's Corner, June 22, 2025, 2:48 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lago-santa-margarita.webp'} alt="A lake with a small boat, geese on the near shore and a tree, and red-roofed houses on the far side." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lago Santa Margarita, 3:45 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="lights">Lights in December</h2>
  <p>
    In December the coast lights up. On December 13, 2025 we spent the day in Laguna. It started at the <strong>Sawdust Art
    Festival</strong>'s Winter Fantasy and ended at dusk on the Montage lawn, where a tall tree covered in warm white lights
    stood on the grass and a second one glowed down by the cove.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'montage-tree.webp'} alt="A tall Christmas tree covered in warm white lights on a lawn at dusk, with a ring of red flowers at its base." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Montage tree, December 13, 2025, 5:12 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'laguna-cove-tree.webp'} alt="Tall palm trunks in the foreground and a small lit tree among dark trees and a cove below, at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view toward the cove, 5:22 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In Newport the lights are on the water. My wife walked Wangcai along the harbor on December 21, 2025, when the boats
    were strung with lights. On Christmas night we walked around Balboa Island together, where the decorations go well beyond a string
    of lights: on one dock stood a gingerbread house outlined in colored bulbs.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'harbor-boat-lights.webp'} alt="A boat outlined in green, white and blue lights, reflected in the dark harbor water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Newport Harbor, December 21, 2025, 7:27 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'balboa-gingerbread.webp'} alt="A small gingerbread house on a dock, with icing-like trim, candy canes and snowflakes, outlined in multicolored lights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Balboa Island, December 25, 2025, 7:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The season starts early in Orange County. By October 23, 2026 the giant tree was already up at
    <strong>Irvine Spectrum</strong>, and Wangcai sat for a photo in front of it. Two days later the houses near the
    harbor in Newport were dressed for Halloween instead, with inflatable ghosts and a glowing pumpkin.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'spectrum-tree.webp'} alt="Wangcai sitting on a plaza at night in front of a giant decorated Christmas tree, with palms and lit shops behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Irvine Spectrum, October 23, 2026, 7:59 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'halloween-decor.webp'} alt="A house at night decorated for Halloween, with an inflatable ghost in a witch's hat and a glowing jack-o'-lantern in a purple-lit garden; Wangcai is a pale shape at the lower left." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Halloween decorations in Newport, October 25, 2026, 8:08 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Other places near home are already on this site: Irvine's Fourth of July show is in <a href="/misc/fireworks">the
    fireworks post</a>, and UC Irvine is in <a href="/misc/campuses">the campuses post</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="muted"><small>Map built from the photos' locations. Photo locations have been removed from the published
    images. Map data © OpenStreetMap contributors; each stop is placed where its photo was taken, and where we live is
    never marked.</small></p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Some photos in this post are my wife's.</small></p>
